WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.962
Before changing messy data, profile it.

00:00:02.962 --> 00:00:10.782
This code loads the sample sales data and then checks three important areas: missing values, duplicate rows,

00:00:10.782 --> 00:00:12.172
and data types. Click Run.

00:00:12.172 --> 00:00:17.362
The isna method identifies missing values, and sum counts them by column.

00:00:17.362 --> 00:00:20.092
Duplicated identifies rows that repeat exactly.

00:00:20.092 --> 00:00:24.987
Dtypes shows how Pandas currently stores each column.

00:00:24.987 --> 00:00:27.862
This step creates a baseline.

00:00:27.862 --> 00:00:31.512
For example, if we know there are two missing values before cleaning,

00:00:31.512 --> 00:00:38.507
we can verify later whether our cleaning strategy handled them.

00:00:38.507 --> 00:00:43.652
If we remove duplicates, we can compare the number of rows before and after.

00:00:43.652 --> 00:00:49.217
This is especially important in professional work because cleaning should be explainable.

00:00:49.217 --> 00:00:53.602
Instead of saying, I cleaned the file, we should be able to say,

00:00:53.602 --> 00:01:00.357
the original file had five rows, one exact duplicate, one missing amount, one missing city,

00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:07.268
and one invalid date. That kind of description makes the workflow reproducible and easier to review.
